  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Alan Turing, 1936', note: 'Before the first computer existed, Turing defined it: a machine with an infinite tape, a head, and a rule table. Everything computable is computable by this absurdly simple device.' },
    { math: '2 · The Church–Turing thesis', note: 'Every "effective procedure" anyone has ever proposed computes exactly what Turing machines compute. One definition swallowed all of arithmetic machinery.' },
    { math: '3 · Undecidability exists', note: 'The halting problem: no program can decide, for every program, whether it halts. Turing proved it by paradox — feed the detector to itself. Some questions are permanently beyond algorithms.' },
    { math: '4 · P: what machines solve quickly', note: 'Problems solvable in polynomial time — sorting, shortest paths, matrix multiply. The "easy" class, where n² or n³ stays civilised.' },
    { math: '5 · NP: what machines can CHECK quickly', note: 'Sudokus, timetables, protein folding, chip layouts: a proposed answer verifies fast, but finding one seems to require searching exponentially many options.' },
    { math: '6 · P vs NP — the $1M question', note: 'Does checking quickly imply finding quickly? If P = NP, modern cryptography collapses and optimisation becomes trivial. Most believe P ≠ NP; nobody can prove it (yet). One of the seven Millennium Prize Problems.' },
  ],
