<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/expert/computability-and-complexity">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Computability and complexity theory ask the two deepest questions in computer science:
    <strong>what can be computed at all?</strong> and <strong>what can be computed
    efficiently?</strong> The answers are surprising: some problems are provably impossible for
    any computer, and an enormous class of practical problems sits in an unresolved middle —
    checkable fast, possibly never findable fast.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from Turing's tape to the million-dollar question" widget="equationStep" config={comp2Cfg} />

  <h2>Why practitioners should care</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Undecidability is practical:</strong> no tool can ever perfectly detect all viruses, all bugs, or all equivalences between programs. Anyone selling "100% guarantees" is selling past a theorem.</li>
    <li><strong>Reductions classify problems:</strong> if you can transform problem A into problem B, A is at least as hard as B. This is how thousands of problems were shown NP-complete — and how you can recognise one before wasting months on an exact solver.</li>
    <li><strong>NP-hard ≠ impossible:</strong> travelling salesman with 1,000 cities has no known fast exact algorithm — but solvers handle real instances daily using structure, approximation and clever search. Theory sets expectations; engineering bends them.</li>
    <li><strong>Cryptography leans on hardness:</strong> RSA and friends bet their security on problems believed hard (factoring, discrete logs). The complexity-class foundation is what makes "believed" mean anything at all.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Turing, "On Computable Numbers" (1936); Cook (1971), Levin (1973); Sipser, Introduction to the Theory of Computation.</p>
</TopicPage>
